{
  "id": "dukotah-book-matrix-hill",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Number panels as Hill-cipher keys",
  "reader_title": "Can the number squares decode groups of runes?",
  "reader_intro": "Treats six proposed number squares, plus a correction to the page 15 square, as keys that mix groups of rune numbers, then checks their complete-page outputs.",
  "question": "Do six pinned number-panel matrices, or the corrected LP2-15 panel, decode complete LP2 pages by fixed Hill blocks?",
  "reader_question": "Could the manuscript’s number panels be matrices that decrypt the runes?",
  "summary": "The archived six-matrix source replay matches. None of its 1,344 page-or-book outputs passes the declared English-score flag. The LP2-15 4×4 array miscopies one printed cell; correcting that cell also produces no flagged output.",
  "finding": "The best source result scores -6.873 versus a −5.0 flag; its one-cell-corrected 4×4 arm scores at best -6.907. Zero outputs in either grid cross the flag. The complete known-reading plant recovers for all 28 operations; 146/199 and 69/199 complete shuffled searches equal or exceed the respective observed maxima.",
  "limitations": "This is a fixed-matrix, zero-origin, English-score test, not an exhaustive Hill search. The source discards incomplete final blocks, omitting 80 runes across the 4×4 page runs and 116 across the 5×5 page runs. The source’s 4×4 panel cell 820 differs from printed 1820; only that single-cell variant was retested. The source’s separate phi arrays are treated literally. Shuffled maxima are conditional order controls, not a cipher-generation probability.",
  "next_steps": "Any further matrix proposal should state the exact cells, block origin, direction, plaintext representation and a complete held-out reading before inspecting an unsolved output.",
  "outcome": "not-supported",
